<speak>
<p>Our goal is the order restriction. If a real circulant Hadamard matrix has order greater than one, then its order must be four times the square of an odd positive integer. We will prove the square and parity restrictions, then use the deeper oddness result.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>We saw that we can encode the first row by a polynomial. For a row of signs, write h of X as the sum of h sub j times X to the j, with every coefficient positive or negative one. Reverse the exponents to form h star.<break time="1400ms"/>The Hadamard condition gives h times h star equals n in the group ring, where X to the n equals one.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Now choose X equal to one. Reversing the powers does not change the value at one: every power of one, including a negative power, is still one. Both polynomials therefore become the same integer sum, h of one. Substituting into the identity gives n equal to h of one times h star of one, which is h of one squared. Since h of one is an integer, n is a perfect square.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>We will now show that n must be even. Because n is greater than one, take two different rows. Their dot product is zero. Each term is positive one or negative one. Pair every positive contribution with a negative contribution. For the sum to vanish, their counts must agree. There are n terms altogether, so n is even. We now have an even perfect square. Its positive square root must be even too, because an odd integer has an odd square. Write that square root as two u. Then n is the square of two u, which is four u squared.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>There is one more restriction: u must be odd. This uses a deeper argument with the polynomial identity. We accept that result here; the square and parity arguments alone do not prove it. Combining these inputs gives n equal to four u squared, with u an odd positive integer.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>The case u equal to one gives our known example of order four. It remains to rule out u greater than one. We proceed by contradiction. Assume a circulant Hadamard matrix exists of order four u squared, with u odd and greater than one. The smallest remaining value is u equal to three, giving order thirty six. This will guide the remaining argument, while the proof must cover every odd u greater than one. Order one remains the trivial case.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
</speak>
